---
import DocsLayout from '../../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import CommandOutput from '../../../../components/CommandOutput.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI pipelines" activeNav="/guides/cli/integrations/pipelines">
  <h1>Pipelines</h1>
  <p>
    A pipeline is a saved pointer to one CI workflow: which integration, which repository, which
    workflow file, which ref by default. Triggering it dispatches the workflow at the provider
    and creates a build record on this side, so the run shows up in the app's build history.
  </p>

  <h2>Commands</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl pipelines list   --integration-id ID
appmctl pipelines get    --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID
appmctl pipelines create --integration-id ID --name NAME [--config JSON]
appmctl pipelines update --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID [--name NAME] [--config JSON]
appmctl pipelines delete --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID
appmctl pipelines inputs --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID
appmctl pipelines run    --integration-id ID PIPELINE_ID [--inputs JSON] [--ref REF] [--request-uuid UUID]`} />
  <p>
    Every action needs <code>--integration-id</code>, because pipelines are scoped to the
    integration that dispatches them. Find it with
    <a href="/guides/cli/integrations"><code>appmctl integrations list</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h3>List</h3>
  <CommandOutput
    command="appmctl pipelines list --integration-id 019fc794-fba5-727d-85c3-69ebbf55b807"
    capture="pipelines-list"
  />
  <p>
    <code>enabled</code> is worth checking before debugging a trigger that appears to do nothing:
    a disabled pipeline is refused rather than dispatched.
  </p>

  <h3>Show one pipeline</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl pipelines get --integration-id "$INT" 019fc795-326e-716d-801b-607117f050f6

# id:               019fc795-326e-716d-801b-607117f050f6
# name:             Android release
# enabled:          true
# profile_type:     production
# reporting_mode:   automatic
# repository:       acme/customer-app
# workflow:         Release
# workflow_path:    .github/workflows/release.yml
# default_ref:      main
# created_at:       2026-08-03T12:24:31.000000Z
# updated_at:       2026-08-03T12:24:31.000000Z`} />

  <h3>Create and update</h3>
  <p>
    <code>--config</code> carries the provider-side wiring. The repository and workflow ids come
    from the <a href="/guides/cli/integrations">discovery commands</a>, since they are the
    provider's identifiers rather than anything this platform invents:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl pipelines create --integration-id "$INT" \\
  --name 'Android release' \\
  --config '{
    "repository_id": "R_kgDOJdocs1",
    "repository_full_name": "acme/customer-app",
    "workflow_id": "98765432",
    "workflow_path": ".github/workflows/release.yml",
    "default_ref": "main",
    "profile_type": "production"
  }'

appmctl pipelines update --integration-id "$INT" pipe-001 --config '{"default_ref":"release/1.4"}'
appmctl pipelines delete --integration-id "$INT" pipe-001`} />

  <h2>Inputs</h2>
  <p>
    A workflow usually declares inputs. <code>inputs</code> reports what this one expects, which
    saves guessing at names that the provider would reject:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl pipelines inputs --integration-id "$INT" pipe-001

# name     type    required
# -------  ------  --------
# branch   string  true
# profile  string  false`} />

  <h2>Triggering a run</h2>
  <p>
    <code>run</code> dispatches the workflow and prints the build record the platform created,
    so the id you need for <code>builds wait</code> comes straight out of the trigger:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl pipelines run --integration-id "$INT" pipe-001 \\
  --ref main --inputs '{"profile":"production"}'

# build_id:          bld-004
# status:            pending
# reporting_mode:    automatic
# dispatch_status:   dispatched
# external_run_url:  https://github.com/acme/customer-app/actions/runs/1234567
# request_uuid:      33333333-4444-4555-8666-777777777777
# created_at:        2026-08-03`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--ref REF</code></td><td>Branch or tag to build, overriding the pipeline's <code>default_ref</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--inputs JSON</code></td><td>Workflow inputs, at most 25 keys</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--request-uuid UUID</code></td><td>Idempotency key; generated when omitted</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <strong>Reuse the idempotency key on retries.</strong> A CI step that reruns after a network
    blip will otherwise dispatch a second workflow. Deriving it from the CI job id makes the
    retry attach to the same build:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`BUILD_ID=$(
  appmctl --json pipelines run --integration-id "$INT" pipe-001 \\
    --ref "$GITHUB_REF_NAME" \\
    --request-uuid "$(uuidgen)" |
    jq -er '.data.id'
)

appmctl builds wait "$BUILD_ID" --timeout 3600`} />

  <h2>What the run produces</h2>
  <p>
    The build it creates reports in <code>automatic</code> mode, which means the provider owns
    its status and logs. Writing status or logs to it from the CLI is refused with
    <code>409</code> — see <a href="/guides/cli/builds/automated-builds">Automated builds</a>
    for what stays available.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>dispatch_status</code> tells you whether the handover succeeded at all. A build that
    never dispatched has no run at the provider, and that is a credentials or configuration
    problem rather than a failing build.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>Scope</th><th>Team permission</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>list</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>inputs</code></td><td><code>pipelines:read</code></td><td><code>view</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>create</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>delete</code></td><td><code>pipelines:write</code></td><td><code>manage_integrations</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>run</code></td><td><code>pipelines:run</code></td><td><code>run_pipelines</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    A key that only triggers builds needs <code>pipelines:run</code> and
    <code>builds:read</code>, and nothing else.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
